Summary of what is covered and verified:
- **Snapshot layout, atomicity & recovery:** `.staging/<run_id>/` isolation (outside `snap-*`), atomic `.tmp` + `os.fsync` + `os.replace` index commit, `.COMPLETE` sentinel, single `os.rename(2)` publish to fixed-width UTC `snap-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-NNNN`, and explicit startup recovery/reporting for abandoned staging, `.trash/`, unverified `snap-*` trees, and corrupt/half-written indexes.
- **Concurrency & retention:** `fcntl.flock` writer lock (`.lock`), per-snapshot `LOCK_SH` reader leases (`.locks/snap-*.lock`) checked with `LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB` before pruning, `keep_last >= 1` floor, and two-phase `.trash/` retirement before `shutil.rmtree`.
- **Source volatility & incremental granularity:** Single-pass `os.scandir` + `S_ISREG` filtering, open-before-create streaming copy with partial-file cleanup and index exclusion on vanished/unreadable files (identical exit/report contract for scheduler and human callers), whole-file hardlink deduplication via `(st_size, st_mtime_ns, st_ctime_ns, st_mode)` with racy-clean guard and explicitly listed missed-edit classes.
- **Measured performance & scaling bounds:** Benchmarked on this host against `synccli.py` (`6,060` files / `200 MB`, `os.link`, `os.fsync`, `json.dump`/`load`, `tracemalloc`) with extrapolated and conservative cold-cache initial-backup budgets plus explicit bounds over $N, F, D, B, F_\Delta, B_\Delta, K, R_{\text{active}}$.
- **Codebase grounding:** Direct reuse and line-cited overrides across `synccli.py` (`MANIFEST_NAME`, `scan_files`, `file_state`, `build_manifest`, `changed_files`, `collect_stale`, `copy_file`, `sync`) and `snaputil.py` (`list_snapshots`, `prune_snapshots`, `snapshot_disk_usage`, `safe_snapshot_path`, `is_stale`); `python3 -m unittest -v` passes all 6 existing tests in `test_synccli.py`.
